INT. DEVEREAUX NATURAL HISTORY MUSEUM, TAXIDERMY HALL - AFTER HOURS

Glass cases in long, shadowed rows: a snarling bear, a heron on one leg, a family of foxes frozen mid-pounce. A moth circles the one working lamp. Dust lies in a fine gray veil across everything.

MONTAGUE FINCH, sixties, the museum director, stands under the lamp with a gold pocket watch in his hand and a smudge of dust on his cuff. PHILIPPA HOLT, forties, a private detective in a green overcoat, kneels beside the heron's case, shining a penlight on the lock. DASHIELL WREN, thirties, the night guard, stands in the doorway with a baton and a sour expression. XIMENA ROJAS, twenties, the intern, clutches a sheaf of loan forms against her chest. LUDO PETRIE, fifties, the taxidermist, sits on a stool in the corner with a sleeve of his cardigan unraveling in his fingers.

                    PHILIPPA
          The lock wasn't forced.

                    MONTAGUE
          Then someone had a key.

                    DASHIELL
          Only three keys exist. Mine. Mr. Finch's. And the taxidermist's.

                    LUDO
          Mine's in my pocket.

He proves it by pulling out a ring, jingling it, and returning it. Montague turns his watch over in his palm. Philippa lifts the lid of the case, takes out a single feather with a pair of tweezers, and holds it up to the lamp. Dashiell shifts his weight and reaches for a handkerchief. Ximena sets the loan forms on a bench, then picks them up again, as though they might be needed. Ludo winds the loose thread of his cardigan around his finger until it goes white.

                    PHILIPPA
          This isn't a heron feather. It's from an ibis.

                    MONTAGUE
          I beg your pardon?

                    PHILIPPA
          Someone swapped the specimen. The ibis is worth forty times the heron.

                    XIMENA
          I catalogued it last month. It was an ibis, in case nine.

                    DASHIELL
          Case nine is empty.

                    LUDO
          (quietly)
          It was never empty.

Everyone turns to look at him. He lets the thread fall.
